"""        <h2>The Scene <span class="em">🎬</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">The best-paid people you know aren't better at their jobs than you. They're better at this meeting.</p>

        <p>The facts: you joined three years ago at a fair salary. Since then you've absorbed the reporting that used to be a whole job, trained two new hires, and become the person clients ask for by name. Your pay has had two cost-of-living nudges. The market, which you've actually researched, now pays your role noticeably more than you're getting.</p>

        <p>The obstacles are not Ingrid — she's fair, busy, and answerable to a budget. The obstacles are in your own head, and every second-language professional knows them by name: the feeling that asking is greedy, that good work should speak for itself, that the conversation might damage something. So hear the truths that dissolve them. Asking is normal — managers expect it, and budget for it. Work does not speak for itself — it needs a spokesperson, and you're the only candidate. And done properly, the ask doesn't damage the relationship; it upgrades it, because you become someone whose value has been said out loud.</p>

        <p>The shape of the meeting: book it honestly (no ambushes), make the case in changes-since-my-number-was-set (not in years served or rent owed), say the number plainly (ranges are for negotiating down), and — whatever the first answer is — leave with something concrete: the yes, the date, or the written path to yes. "We'll see" is the only losing outcome, and it only happens if you accept it.</p>

        <ol class="bigq">
            <li>Why does asking for money feel shameful when working for money doesn't? Where did you learn that?</li>
            <li>"Good work speaks for itself." Who benefits most from you believing that?</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="whos">
            <div class="who"><b>You</b><span>Three years, expanded role, market research done, number chosen. Nervous — and going in anyway, which is the whole trick.</span></div>
            <div class="who"><b>Ingrid</b><span>Your manager. Fair, direct, guards a budget, answers to her own boss — and privately respects people who make this case well.</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="tip"><b>First:</b> read the conversation and notice what the case is built from: changes since the salary was set — never years, never loyalty, never personal costs. Then find the moment You refuses to accept "we'll see" — politely, and completely.</div>""",

